    12	# Design analysis for the D1 variant
    13	# a) Concreteization + behavior trace:
    14	#    The visual scene stays the same at a high level: a thin grey trail connects exactly
    15	#    two destination sprites, while 2-3 other bordered sprites sit elsewhere. The change
    16	#    is only in how a destination chooses its source. In the original task a destination
    17	#    with border 2 and center 7 would search for a source whose border is 7. In this
    18	#    variant the same destination must search for a source whose border is also 2, then
    19	#    copy that source's center into the destination. To make this visible, each example
    20	#    contains two repeated border colors: each destination border color appears once on
    21	#    the path-connected destination and once on an unconnected source. Destination center
    22	#    colors are chosen from colors that never appear as borders, so the old cross-attribute
    23	#    lookup would fail while the new same-attribute lookup succeeds.
    24	# b) Complexity calibration:
    25	#    Repeating exactly two border colors is enough support for the new rule without making
    26	#    the grid noisy. Allowing many duplicates would create ambiguous pairings; keeping all
    27	#    borders unique would make the new rule degenerate. The generator therefore uses
    28	#    exactly two destination-source pairs plus an optional unique-border distractor.
    29	# c) Hidden assumptions from the original main():
    30	#    The original border lookup implicitly relied on border colors being unique, so
    31	#    including the destination itself in `by_border[d.center]` was harmless. Under the
    32	#    new rule `by_border[d.border]` would always include the destination itself and would
    33	#    incorrectly self-match unless non-destinations are filtered out. The new solver builds
    34	#    its border lookup from non-destination sprites only, and the generator guarantees
    35	#    exactly one non-destination sprite for each destination border color.
    36	# d) Placement legality:
    37	#    The output-first construction is preserved. Destinations are placed first, then the
    38	#    grey path is drawn between their valid port cells. Afterwards the input is obtained by
    39	#    restoring destination centers and placing source/distractor sprites in empty space.
    40	#    Non-destination sprites are rejected if they would overlap, 4-touch another sprite, or
    41	#    expose one of the two grey endpoints as one of their own ports; this keeps endpoint
    42	#    ownership unique so destination selection remains valid.
    43	# e) Visual observability:
    44	#    The relevant clue is now "same border color" rather than "destination center equals
    45	#    some other border." Because destination centers are deliberately outside the border
    46	#    palette, a human can see that center colors are not the pairing cue anymore. The
    47	#    repeated outlines are the repeated evidence, similar in spirit to same-attribute color
